  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...Continuing to see the \nwelcome break for the panhandle for this weekend. Most of the \nforecast thoughts are unchanged for this weekend period. A \nbuilding ridge looks to keep a majority of the precipitation to \nthe west, and with no stronger system, this ridge does not look to\nbreak down easily. Therefore, expect to see a cooling trend going\ninto the weekend, only due to some cloud cover breaking up and \nradiative cooling dropping most of the panhandle below freezing \novernight. Not looking to see significantly below normal \ntemperatures overnight; however, as 850 mb temperatures still \nlooks significantly above normal for this time of year. May also \nhave some fog chances during the overnight period which could \nextend into the daytime period. If any fog does develop this \nweekend, then daytime temperatures may not exceed 40 degrees. \nTherefore, the temperature forecast has become significantly more \nuncertain. \n\nGoing into the work week next week, the marine threat returns.\nModels still are struggling to determine how much of the ridge\naloft will be broken down from the low pressure moving up from \nthe south. Leaning harder into the EC and Canadian solutions as\nensemble clustering has a majority of members keeping the ridge in\nthe northern panhandle longer. While this is just what the\nofficial, deterministic forecast represents, there is a smaller\nchance, <30%, which also includes the deterministic GFS, which\ngoes for a much stronger solution. This solution has widespread\ngales and storm force wind chances in the eastern gulf.",
